WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:04.750
While watching David Letterman’s new show
on Netflix I started to feel something was

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:05.750
off.

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:08.590
The host and the guest were great, but I felt
disconnected from the interview.

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:11.540
Watch this and pay attention to the editing
and cinematography:

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:17.790
The camera is literally spinning and jumping
all around the host and the guest.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:42.820
It feels like a sequence of shots more suited
to an action scene than a 

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:47.380
sit down interview.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.750
The interview format is age old.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.470
Most interviews hold to the same general shape.

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:56.399
There’s a wide shot that shows both the
interviewer and the guest, and a closer single

00:00:56.399 --> 00:00:58.000
on each of them.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.790
When a guest starts telling a story on a talk
show, the camera usually holds on them from

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:06.859
one angle as much as possible, cutting only
when necessary and to show the host’s reaction.

00:01:06.859 --> 00:01:10.729
When the camera finally holds on a shot for
more than a few seconds on My Next Guest,

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:15.020
I feel relieved just to have a moment to catch
up and relax.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:18.750
It seems like the creators of My Next Guest
were interested in setting themselves apart

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:19.920
from the late night genre.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.350
It’s clear they want to innovate on the
interview format.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:27.330
But when a format has been established for
as long as the interview format, there’s

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:30.130
a reason why it’s endured mostly unchanged.

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:31.369
It works.

00:01:31.369 --> 00:01:33.930
The problem with My Next Guest is twofold.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:36.349
The first is that there are too many camera
angles.

00:01:36.349 --> 00:01:39.520
For every new angle we’re more likely to
notice the cut.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.170
My Next Guest uses so many that you never
acclimate to all the angles, so even 30 minutes

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:49.380
into the interview some cuts appear novel,
drawing attention back to the editing and

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:50.380
cinematography.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:55.169
In an interview the information we’re interested
in is simply what the host and guest are saying

00:01:55.169 --> 00:01:57.569
both verbally and non-verbally.

00:01:57.569 --> 00:02:03.009
The sooner the cinematography becomes invisible
the better we can focus on that communication.

00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:05.520
The second half of the problem is the editing.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.619
The number of camera angles isn’t a huge
issue on it’s own.

00:02:08.619 --> 00:02:11.200
You could have that many camera angles and
use them effectively.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.980
But the cutting between these angles often
follows no discernible pattern or logic.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:18.530
It certainly doesn’t follow the natural
rhythm of the conversation.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:22.530
When you’re making arbitrary cuts to arbitrary
angles, we begin to notice the editing and

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:30.290
the camera work, drawing us away from the
content of the interview.

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:40.840
If the goal during an interview is for the
editing to disappear, then there are three

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.610
functional ways we can use a cut.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:47.790
The first is to show relevant information,
this could be a reaction from the host, or

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:50.100
a cut to show the host while they’re speaking.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:58.010
The second is using a cut as punctuation or
emphasis for what the guest or host is saying.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:08.260
The 

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:13.830
third is to edit out mistakes, or awkward
pauses to improve the flow of the conversation.

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:18.040
Let’s look at an example from Seinfeld’s
Comedians in Cars getting coffee, a show that

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:40.430
I think has effectively innovated on the interview
format.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:44.570
Notice how this clip uses a variety of angles
and cuts at a frequency similar to My Next

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:45.570
Guest.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:47.170
But each cut and angle are meaningful.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:48.690
Let’s break it down.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:54.740
The first two shots form the setup and punchline
of a joke.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:04:00.930
The division of the two shots help punctuate
the joke and the cut to the second angle also

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:03.640
gives us a better view of Seinfeld’s response.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:12.580
The second shot also serves as the setup for
a second punch line, which comes right after

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:13.580
the third cut.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:19.910
Then the last two shots do the same thing.

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:33.970
What’s important here is that a clear rhythm
gets established.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:55.500
Now compare that to this clip from Letterman’s
show.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:03.889
The 
editing serves only to distract.

00:05:03.889 --> 00:05:07.919
The first two shots contain one complete though
that doesn’t need to be divided.

00:05:07.919 --> 00:05:15.469
The 
second cut makes sense, as it acts as punctuation

00:05:15.469 --> 00:05:38.060
for the sentence, but the one that follows
that is awkward and unnecessary.

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:42.199
We can see that these cuts aren’t being
made to cover up mistakes or to edit the conversation,

00:05:42.199 --> 00:05:46.120
because if they were, the editor would have
removed Obama’s uhh here.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:51.130
The cut following that focus on Letterman,
but he’s not reacting in a way that’s

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:54.039
interesting and relevant, he’s just rubbing
his nose.

00:05:54.039 --> 00:05:57.800
The cut back to Obama is fine, but we never
should have cut away in the first place.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:03.050
I’m highlight a particularly bad clip, there’s
sections of My Next Guest where the editing

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:04.210
is fine.

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:08.240
But there’s plenty of sections where the
editing and cinematography come across as

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:09.879
a weak attempt to spice things up.

00:06:09.879 --> 00:06:12.639
There’s a lot that’s great about Letterman’s
new show.

00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:17.309
Letterman himself is a fantastic interviewer,
he knows how to probe gently, and he’s sympathetic.

00:06:17.309 --> 00:06:21.499
I love the documentary elements added to the
interviews, they help ground the guest’s

00:06:21.499 --> 00:06:22.499
stories in history.

00:06:22.499 --> 00:06:27.430
But throwing more camera angles and fast cutting
to an old format rarely improves things, and

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:30.569
it shows us that sometimes, more is less.

00:06:30.569 --> 00:06:35.449
I think the producers of the show need to
have more faith that the host and guest are

00:06:35.449 --> 00:06:44.900
interesting enough to hold the audience’s
attention without flashy cinematography and

00:06:44.900 --> 00:07:02.310
editing.

